"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[6125],{46125:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('{"id":"base-02","slug":"base-02","trackId":"base","order":3,"title":"Security First","subtitle":"Clear Rules for Protecting Capital","estimatedMinutes":7,"access":"free","quizCount":3,"blueprintCount":0,"trainerIds":["HUB-CALC-08"],"markdown":"Lesson 2 \xb7 4 min\\n\\n## Security\\n\\nSecurity First: Everyone Wants to Trick You\\n\\nCryptocurrency gives people more control over their money, but also more responsibility. If your bank-card details are stolen, you may be able to block the transaction, recover the money or contact support. Cryptocurrency often works differently.\\n\\nIf you send funds to a scammer, sign a malicious transaction or give someone wallet access, recovering the money may be impossible. The first rule is therefore simple:\\n\\nsecurity first, everything else afterwards.\\n\\n### Why Crypto Attracts So Many Scammers\\n\\nThe reason is simple: money can be transferred quickly, directly and often irreversibly. A scammer does not need to hack the blockchain. It is much easier to deceive you, persuading you to open a fake link.\\n\\nThey may impersonate support, promise easy money, ask for your wallet’s seed phrase or exploit fear and greed.\\n\\nMost crypto fraud therefore targets the person rather than the technology.\\n\\n### Do Not Trust Unsolicited Direct Messages\\n\\nImagine reporting a wallet problem in Telegram or Discord, then receiving a message from a “support agent” a few minutes later. This is very likely a scammer. They may use the project’s logo, a similar name or even a real employee’s photograph. Next, they ask you to open a link, connect a wallet or enter information.\\n\\nTreat anyone who contacts you first through a direct message as a potential scammer.\\n\\n### A Famous Name Is No Guarantee\\n\\nA large following guarantees nothing. Accounts can be hacked, videos fabricated and Telegram channels copied.\\n\\n“A famous person posted it” proves nothing by itself. If someone asks you to send money, connect a wallet or buy urgently, pause and verify the information.\\n\\n### Check the Website\\n\\nFake websites can look almost identical to genuine ones: the same images, logo and buttons.\\n\\nSometimes the only difference is one letter in the address. Be particularly careful with links from Telegram, Discord, comments, advertisements, emails and direct messages. Access familiar services through their official website or a bookmark saved beforehand.\\n\\n### Free Money Is Almost Never Free\\n\\nCrypto regularly features messages such as:\\n\\n“You have won tokens.”\\n\\nAn offer that looks too good to be true is probably fraudulent, especially when someone rushes you:\\n\\n“Today only.” “10 minutes left.” “Claim now or lose the reward.” The purpose is to make you stop thinking and start acting.\\n\\n### Do Not Rush\\n\\nMost scams create a sense of urgency. When money is involved, rushing rarely helps. Before acting:\\n- stop and think critically;\\n- give yourself time to consider everything;\\n- make sure you understand what is happening;\\n- check the website again;\\n- check the recipient;\\n- read what you are confirming.\\n\\nFive minutes of checking can save all your money.\\n\\n### Nobody Should Know Your Seed Phrase\\n\\nA seed phrase is the master key to your wallet. It usually contains 12 or 24 words. Anyone who obtains it effectively gains complete control of your funds. It does not matter whether they claim to be support, a project administrator or a famous blogger.\\n\\nNever send your seed phrase to anyone. Ever.\\n\\nReal support will never request it.\\n\\n### Check the Address Before Sending\\n\\nCryptocurrency addresses are long and look something like this:\\n\\n0x71C...8F3A\\n\\nPeople usually copy them, which makes mistakes easy. You could paste the wrong address or accidentally send money to someone else. Compare the address characters before transferring. For repeat transfers, avoid quick access to “recent addresses”; verify everything manually and control the process.\\n\\nScammers regularly send transactions to addresses to appear in the list of addresses you supposedly interacted with. If you are sending a large amount for the first time, send a small test amount first.\\n\\n### Do Not Sign What You Do Not Understand\\n\\nSometimes a wallet asks you not to send money, but merely to “sign” something. It may look harmless. However, a signature may grant an application certain rights over your assets. If you do not understand the website, why it requests confirmation or which rights you are granting, do not confirm anything.\\n\\n### Nobody Will Refund Your Money in Crypto\\n\\nIf you confirm a transfer to the wrong address, a bank cannot press “cancel.” If you give a scammer wallet access, there is nobody to compensate you. The central security rule is therefore:\\n\\nstay alert and trust nobody.\\n\\nSafe or Scam? Seven Situations\\n\\nYou complain about a wallet in a chat; a minute later, “support” sends you a direct message.\\n\\n### What to Remember\\n- Never share your seed phrase.\\n- Do not open suspicious links.\\n- Do not trust strangers who contact you first through direct messages.\\n- Check the website and recipient addresses.\\n- Do not confirm actions you do not understand.\\n- Do not believe promises of guaranteed returns or free money.\\n- Before a large transfer, send a test amount first.\\n- Do not make financial decisions in a hurry.\\n\\nMost importantly:\\n\\nyou do not need to be paranoid in crypto. You need to make checking a habit.\\n\\nThe next article explains how to store cryptocurrency correctly and what a cryptocurrency wallet actually is.\\n\\n### Check Yourself 1 / 3\\n\\nA support agent contacts you first and asks for your seed phrase to restore your wallet. What should you do?\\n\\n### Terms Used in This Lesson:\\n- **Blockchain** — A record of all transactions that everyone can inspect: entries can be added, but old entries cannot be changed. It is stored simultaneously on millions of computers, making falsification of the history physically impossible\\n- **Yield** — The annual percentage an investment actually earns at today’s price. A bond’s price and yield move in opposite directions: when the bond becomes cheaper, its yield rises. Government bond yields influence loan and deposit rates\\n- **Seed Phrase** — 12 or 24 words used to restore a wallet. Whoever has the phrase controls the money, so neither support staff nor project administrators need to ask for it\\n- **Token** — A digital asset that exists within a network, such as USDT or USDC. The same token can exist on different networks, and those are different versions of it\\n\\n### End-of-Lesson Quiz (“Check Yourself”):\\n**Question 1: A support agent contacts you first and asks for your seed phrase to restore your wallet. What should you do?**\\n- ⚪ Option 1: Share the phrase if the support account looks official\\n- ⚪ Option 2: Share only half the words\\n- ✅ **[CORRECT ANSWER]** Option 3: Never share the seed phrase with anyone\\n\\n**Question 2: You are told to open a link urgently and confirm a transaction or “the money will disappear in 10 minutes.” What is safest?**\\n- ⚪ Option 1: Confirm quickly before the deadline\\n- ✅ **[CORRECT ANSWER]** Option 2: Stop and independently verify the website, information and action\\n- ⚪ Option 3: Forward the link to a friend and confirm if it opens\\n\\n**Question 3: Why is it often easier for scammers to deceive a user than hack the blockchain?**\\n- ✅ **[CORRECT ANSWER]** Option 1: The user may voluntarily hand over access or confirm a malicious operation\\n- ⚪ Option 2: Blockchain has no security at all\\n- ⚪ Option 3: All crypto wallets use the same passwords\\n\\n\\n---","blueprints":[],"quiz":[{"id":"q-base-02-1","prompt":"A support agent contacts you first and asks for your seed phrase to restore your wallet. What should you do?","options":[{"id":"base-02-1-1","label":"Share the phrase if the support account looks official","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. Never share the seed phrase with anyone"},{"id":"base-02-1-2","label":"Share only half the words","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. Never share the seed phrase with anyone"},{"id":"base-02-1-3","label":"Never share the seed phrase with anyone","isCorrect":true,"feedback":"Correct. Never share the seed phrase with anyone"}],"explanation":"Never share the seed phrase with anyone","sourceRef":"CAPITAL_BASE:451–454 (Verbatim)"},{"id":"q-base-02-2","prompt":"You are told to open a link urgently and confirm a transaction or “the money will disappear in 10 minutes.” What is safest?","options":[{"id":"base-02-2-1","label":"Confirm quickly before the deadline","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. Stop and independently verify the website, information and action"},{"id":"base-02-2-2","label":"Stop and independently verify the website, information and action","isCorrect":true,"feedback":"Correct. Stop and independently verify the website, information and action"},{"id":"base-02-2-3","label":"Forward the link to a friend and confirm if it opens","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. Stop and independently verify the website, information and action"}],"explanation":"Stop and independently verify the website, information and action","sourceRef":"CAPITAL_BASE:456–459 (Verbatim)"},{"id":"q-base-02-3","prompt":"Why is it often easier for scammers to deceive a user than hack the blockchain?","options":[{"id":"base-02-3-1","label":"The user may voluntarily hand over access or confirm a malicious operation","isCorrect":true,"feedback":"Correct. The user may voluntarily hand over access or confirm a malicious operation"},{"id":"base-02-3-2","label":"Blockchain has no security at all","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. The user may voluntarily hand over access or confirm a malicious operation"},{"id":"base-02-3-3","label":"All crypto wallets use the same passwords","isCorrect":false,"feedback":"Incorrect. The user may voluntarily hand over access or confirm a malicious operation"}],"explanation":"The user may voluntarily hand over access or confirm a malicious operation","sourceRef":"CAPITAL_BASE:461–464 (Verbatim)"}],"source":{"file":"00_FREE_GIFT_MakeCash_Crypto_And_Capital_Base_15_Lessons.md","sha256":"88a3efb162434bb8a9b3b000caee579d9c27005a8ea46ddf4ef9cafb50dc45cc","lineStart":325,"lineEnd":469}}')}}]);